Collision geometry fluctuations and triangular flow in heavy-ion collisions
abstract: We introduce the concepts of participant triangularity and triangular flow in heavy-ion collisions, analogous to the definitions of participant eccentricity and elliptic flow. The participant triangularity characterizes the triangular anisotropy of the initial nuclear overlap geometry and arises from event-by-event fluctuations in the participant-nucleon collision points. In studies using a multi-phase transport model (AMPT), a triangular flow signal is observed that is proportional to the participant triangularity and corresponds to a large third Fourier coefficient in two-particle azimuthal correlation functions. Using two-particle azimuthal correlations at large pseudorapidity separations measured by the PHOBOS and STAR experiments, we show that this Fourier component is also present in data. Ratios of the second and third Fourier coefficients in data exhibit similar trends as a function of centrality and transverse momentum as in AMPT calculations. These findings suggest a significant contribution of triangular flow to the ridge and broad away-side features observed in data. Triangular flow provides a new handle on the initial collision geometry and collective expansion dynamics in heavy-ion collisions.
Introduction: Studies of two-particle azimuthal correlations have become a key tool in characterizing the evolution of the strongly interacting medium formed in ultra-relativistic nucleus-nucleus collisions. Traditionally, the observed two-particle azimuthal correlation structures are thought to arise from two distinct contributions. The dominant one is the “elliptic flow” term, related to anisotropic hydrodynamic expansion of the medium from an anisotropic initial state , , , , , , , , . In addition, one observes so-called “non-flow” contributions from, e.g., resonances and jets, which may be modified by their interactions with the medium , , .The strength of anisotropic flow is usually quantified with a Fourier decomposition of the azimuthal distribution of observed particles relative to the reaction plane . The experimental observable related to elliptic flow is the second Fourier coefficient, “{{formula:d7651660-6642-4a9c-b4c5-26b7ce3b5e6f}} .” The elliptic flow signal has been studied extensively in Au+Au collisions at RHIC as a function of pseudorapidity, centrality, transverse momentum, particle species and center of mass energy , , , , . The centrality and transverse momentum dependence of {{formula:68f195aa-71a2-48be-a93e-eb8afd9a5a64}} has been found to be well described by hydrodynamic calculations, which for a given equation of state, can be used to relate a given initial energy density distribution to final momentum distribution of produced particles . In these calculations, the {{formula:1c5183e2-4d0c-41b2-a296-2e4f8cb5a426}} signal is found to be proportional to the eccentricity, {{formula:6bc7fa6c-7f47-4191-ab06-44261dfff12c}} , of the initial collision region defined by the overlap of the colliding nuclei . Detailed comparisons of the observed elliptic flow effects with hydrodynamic calculations have led to the conclusion that a new state of strongly interacting matter with very low shear viscosity, compared to its entropy density, has been created in these collisions , , , .Measurements of non-flow correlations in heavy-ion collisions, in comparison to corresponding studies in p+p collisions, provide information on particle production mechanisms and parton-medium interactions , , . Different methods have been developed to account for the contribution of elliptic flow to two-particle correlations in these studies of the underlying non-flow correlations , , , , . The most commonly used approach is the zero yield at minimum method (ZYAM), where one assumes that the associated particle yield correlated with the trigger particle is zero at the minimum as a function of {{formula:e78b957c-bea8-419a-b420-db6a24177a69}} after elliptic flow contribution is taken out . The ZYAM approach has yielded rich correlation structures at {{formula:3d6ad0e4-7a78-4366-9220-ddebfb00ffb3}} and {{formula:aa7e6566-e9c6-4f58-857a-09fdbc85f2ee}} for different {{formula:52f533f1-a570-4da2-90ae-488a15c1fb2b}} ranges , , , . These structures, which are not observed in p+p collisions at the same collision energy, have been referred to as the “ridge” and “broad away-side” or “shoulder”. The same correlation structures have been found to be present in Pb+Au collisions at {{formula:08da4712-b962-4c89-a73c-5567fbdc1021}} at the SPS . Measurements at RHIC have shown that these structures extend out to large pseudorapidity separations of {{formula:cea09421-ed25-4abb-a347-f43cbd1f7ce8}} , similar to elliptic flow correlations . The ridge and broad away-side structures have been extensively studied experimentally , , , , , and various theoretical models have been proposed to understand their origin , , , , , , , . A recent review of the theoretical and experimental results can be found in . {{figure:16b7e1df-33c3-47b9-b20e-bce4f9a58136}}In this paper, we propose that the observed ridge and broad away-side features in two-particle correlations may be due to an average triangular anisotropy in the initial collision geometry which is caused by event-by-event fluctuations and which leads to a triangular anisotropy in azimuthal particle production through the collective expansion of the medium. It was shown that, in the NEXSPHERIO hydrodynamic model, ridge and broad away-side structures in two particle correlations arise if non-smooth initial conditions are introduced . Sorensen has suggested that fluctuations of the initial collision geometry may lead to higher order Fourier components in the azimuthal correlation function through collective effects . An analysis of higher order components in the Fourier decomposition of azimuthal particle distributions, including the odd terms, was proposed by Mishra et al. to probe superhorizon fluctuations in the thermalization stage . In this work, we show that the second and third Fourier components of two-particle correlations may be best studied by treating the components of corresponding initial geometry fluctuations on equal footing. To reduce contributions of non-flow correlations, which are most prominent in short pseudorapidity separations, we focus on azimuthal correlations at long ranges in pseudorapidity. We show that the ridge and broad away-side structures can be well described by the first three coefficients of a Fourier expansion of the azimuthal correlation function {{formula:0422b451-cde0-40fc-8a7a-215b83d6cfaf}}where the first component, {{formula:a582bd88-b984-4c50-804d-3f1c7de0bdd9}}Note the distinction between {{formula:52c32192-5812-4063-86da-28361adc6800}} and {{formula:a3b1f565-f11d-42eb-ac11-bbcb47089586}} . See Eqs. REF and REF for details., is understood to be due to momentum conservation and directed flow and the second component {{formula:706c2636-856f-4b06-a5a1-1d2d36e9c028}} is dominated by the contribution from elliptic flow. Studies in a multi-phase transport model (AMPT) suggest that not only the elliptic flow term, {{formula:972f3151-94d4-4c73-a0f3-2ffc462b6829}} , but also a large part of the correlations measured by the {{formula:16f7fb7d-a947-4028-8fb4-6f8ba49306d5}} term, arises from the hydrodynamic expansion of the medium. {{figure:327ee470-24fb-4cb2-917d-b4656690ef4c}}{{figure:c03284da-371a-42d0-abd6-5d0b36f67bba}}
Fourier decomposition of azimuthal correlations: In the existing correlation data, different correlation measures such as {{formula:3724ac23-9013-4203-afea-651e88a93bb2}} , {{formula:2052605b-64c3-4296-97cc-7619767945ec}} and {{formula:992e6b9a-717b-46f6-afbd-ec7087623233}} have been used to study different sources of particle correlations. The azimuthal projection of all of these correlation functions have the form {{formula:b5383366-7714-4096-af69-1712ca47b577}}where the scale factor {{formula:16499782-e29c-4984-a900-24f4ade645ee}} and offset {{formula:aa59b466-343e-4324-b25e-231143937bde}} depend on the definition of the correlation function as well as the pseudorapidity range of the projection . Examples of long range azimuthal correlation distributions are shown in Fig. REF for mid-central Au+Au collisions with different trigger and associated particle {{formula:5ca6ba89-dd72-47e2-b2b1-d1c4a56bcf6f}} selections obtained by projecting the two-dimensional correlation functions onto the {{formula:19be9971-f6ed-4052-8736-125410170b7c}} axis at pseudorapidity separations of {{formula:3ba44111-e7bb-4adb-8b5f-43a451e7d064}} for STAR data and {{formula:1730a8bd-2ddc-4356-97fe-8b65d4e43729}} for PHOBOS data , . The correlation function data used in this study are available at , , . Also shown in Fig. REF are the first three Fourier components of the azimuthal correlations and the residual after these components are taken out. The data is found to be very well described by the three Fourier components. {{figure:5f2668c3-4b88-4294-8f81-164794119151}}
Participant triangularity and triangular flow: It is useful to recall that traditional hydrodynamic calculations start from a smooth matter distribution given by the transverse overlap of two Woods-Saxon distributions. In such calculations, elliptic flow is aligned with the orientation of the reaction plane defined by the impact parameter direction and the beam axis and by symmetry, no {{formula:1a857adb-12a5-46bb-92d5-ce7dcf083214}} component arises in the azimuthal correlation function. To describe this component in terms of hydrodynamic flow requires a revised understanding of the initial collision geometry, taking into account fluctuations in the nucleon-nucleon collision points from event to event. The possible influence of initial geometry fluctuations was used to explain the surprisingly large values of elliptic flow measured for central Cu+Cu collision, where the average eccentricity calculated with respect to the reaction plane angle is small . For a Glauber Monte Carlo event, the minor axis of eccentricity of the region defined by nucleon-nucleon interaction points does not necessarily point along the reaction plane vector, but may be tilted. The “participant eccentricity” , calculated with respect to this tilted axis is found to be finite even for most central events and significantly larger than the reaction plane eccentricity for the smaller Cu+Cu system. Following this idea, event-by-event elliptic flow fluctuations have been measured and found to be consistent with the expected fluctuations in the initial state geometry with the new definition of eccentricity . In this paper, we use this method of quantifying the initial anisotropy exclusively.Mathematically, the participant eccentricity is given as {{formula:ede06310-2a55-44f8-9c9e-e75562b19a91}}where {{formula:b49e4753-3c49-46d3-bd1e-785aa6ac93e3}} , {{formula:6d5eb34e-16b8-476c-bc55-24407e1f75c3}} and {{formula:08a4c86f-98c5-4124-8016-cae1788a5f57}} , are the event-by-event (co)variances of the participant nucleon distributions along the transverse directions {{formula:6824bb67-4d27-4445-97d9-588e73b4312a}} and {{formula:bbb9c885-3395-42da-b5ff-3d39867be068}} . If the coordinate system is shifted to the center of mass of the participating nucleons such that {{formula:6ba5ae16-a0e3-4703-988a-210bb879143b}} , it can be shown that the definition of eccentricity is equivalent to {{formula:f1abb0cf-4150-4a21-8f3c-2dbeccf30d92}}in this shifted frame, where {{formula:f4f498bf-1f8f-4877-8c1e-4a8495c2094f}} and {{formula:c0d8db20-4393-4685-bc4b-c00beba944ef}} are the polar coordinate positions of participating nucleons. The minor axis of the ellipse defined by this region is given as {{formula:4ac5a811-676d-4340-ae19-42cfd57577f8}}Since the pressure gradients are largest along {{formula:7d0e6641-8795-4e94-9289-2eccf969a561}} , the collective flow is expected to be the strongest in this direction. The definition of {{formula:7313276b-6a50-420d-8308-a6f059f65771}} has conceptually changed to refer to the second Fourier coefficient of particle distribution with respect to {{formula:f4fdb765-764d-41cd-8c73-5ccf91340a03}} rather than the reaction plane {{formula:d12954cc-3278-487d-bb92-363a2177b04b}}This change has not impacted the experimental definition since the directions of the reaction plane angle or {{formula:5ae13ea5-5b3c-4edc-bc86-deb925578348}} are not a priori known.Drawing an analogy to eccentricity and elliptic flow, the initial and final triangular anisotropies can be quantified as participant triangularity, {{formula:3f0572f0-a605-4fef-bf66-a37db71b95f7}} , and triangular flow, {{formula:e7279e52-4a86-4143-ae84-c9d8957a66b2}} , respectively: {{formula:dac9aeb5-9991-4484-9c2e-ad2050c1445c}}where {{formula:7693e4fb-09dc-4041-9980-4856c1c7f5b3}} is the minor axis of participant triangularity given by {{formula:bd32a475-587f-4769-8de3-88a44f322869}}It is important to note that the minor axis of triangularity is found to be uncorrelated with the reaction plane angle and the minor axis of eccentricity in Glauber Monte Carlo calculations. This implies that the average triangularity calculated with respect to the reaction plane angle or {{formula:d78ec1a3-7f9e-4093-90af-0c265f41700b}} is zero. The participant triangularity defined in Eq. REF , however, is calculated with respect to {{formula:5915c97b-1c52-4222-b05b-4b624b44c788}} and is always finite. {{figure:d0137458-80ef-4e07-acc8-7d8ea2ea4bd2}}The distributions of eccentricity and triangularity calculated with the PHOBOS Glauber Monte Carlo implementation for Au+Au events at {{formula:02bd9958-27f8-44d9-9802-da1264c2f9a5}} 200 GeV are shown in Fig. REF . The value of triangularity is observed to fluctuate event-by-event and have an average magnitude of the same order as eccentricity. Transverse distribution of nucleons for a sample Monte Carlo event with a high value of triangularity is shown in Fig. REF . A clear triangular anisotropy can be seen in the region defined by the participating nucleons.
Triangular flow in the AMPT model: To assess the connection between triangularity and the ridge and broad away-side features in two-particle correlations, we study elliptic and triangular flow in the AMPT model. AMPT is a hybrid model which consists of four main components: initial conditions, parton cascade, string fragmentation, and A Relativistic Transport Model for hadrons. The model successfully describes main features of the dependence of elliptic flow on centrality and transverse momentum . Ridge and broad away-side features in two-particle correlations are also observed in the AMPT model , . Furthermore, the dependence of quantitative observables such as away-side RMS width and away-side splitting parameter {{formula:9680d1f5-cae5-4205-8ea3-0b2b28fb7d9e}} on transverse momentum and reaction plane in AMPT reproduces the experimental results successfully, where a ZYAM-based elliptic flow subtraction is applied to both the data and the model , .The initial conditions of AMPT are obtained from Heavy Ion Jet Interaction Generator (HIJING) . HIJING uses a Glauber Model implementation that is similar to the PHOBOS implementation to determine positions of participating nucleons. It is possible to calculate the values of {{formula:9a283f94-52c2-49ed-a4f2-e0c419fb0390}} , {{formula:d350f448-591f-44f9-bf36-711ba4344351}} , {{formula:f5dc8ed8-3729-4b04-bc76-38631ef74a81}} and {{formula:9455ddbf-f8c5-45cf-94a4-b45c362b11b1}} event-by-event from the positions of these nucleons [see Equations REF , REF , REF and REF ]. Next, we calculate the magnitudes of elliptic and triangular flow with respect to {{formula:635f62d1-71bf-4efb-acd2-ca46559ee40f}} and {{formula:5554408e-e238-4887-ac94-a8e39bc3814f}} respectively as defined in Eqs. REF and .The average value of elliptic flow, {{formula:97d466be-ffe4-4c01-9e0b-1029d282440c}} , and triangular flow, {{formula:95e9765f-6735-4d94-8250-93f5854736d9}} , for particles in the pseudorapidity range {{formula:7cd14f63-7974-489b-8e42-50e07abe5fdf}} in {{formula:676c00fa-edd3-498c-8d27-64c132359c27}} 200 GeV Au+Au collisions from AMPT are shown as a function of {{formula:8be831ee-f163-4d1a-9219-7f7df81985ee}} and {{formula:6cdfc71e-4021-4575-a30a-e84e8bee5d6e}} in Fig. REF for different ranges of number of participating nucleons. As previously expected, the magnitude of {{formula:d737306a-c5c2-4330-a878-179a7e24aa9d}} is found to be proportional to {{formula:36ead482-f4a0-4e31-9dcd-029ef1fbec8c}} . We observe that a similar linear relation is also present between triangular flow and triangularity. {{figure:9983c0db-62d1-4b3e-bc33-232465417e50}}{{figure:373dd258-82c5-4673-94bb-2211923fbf50}}After establishing that triangular anisotropy in initial collision geometry leads to a triangular anisotropy in particle production, we investigate the contribution of triangular flow to the observed ridge and broad away-side features in two-particle azimuthal correlations. For a given pseudorapidity window, the Fourier coefficients of two-particle azimuthal correlations, {{formula:f0545cbe-10ba-4b72-a4d2-24cfcc2fb228}} , can be calculated in AMPT by averaging {{formula:86519b49-8b29-489f-aba1-b5135312bc72}} over all particle pairs. Contributions from elliptic (triangular) flow is present in the second (third) Fourier coefficient of {{formula:71299cd3-5357-47bc-ac75-1aa29112f307}} distribution since {{formula:8fc847bd-24ec-4702-8c71-e0438aecf2d6}}For a given pseudorapidity window, this contribution can be calculated from average elliptic (triangular) flow values as {{formula:946395f1-53a2-4ffd-9511-0b7c7366c155}}where {{formula:983cdddc-c373-4cfe-b65f-5a2235f6b32c}} ({{formula:09800d4b-b8ca-43cb-9bfd-9c428ded4ed8}} ) and the integration is over the pseudorapidity range of particle pairs. The average single-particle distribution coefficients, {{formula:422117bb-0347-4132-ae9c-0eb91c449265}} , are used in this calculation to avoid contributions from non-flow correlations which may be present if the two-particle distributions, {{formula:6ff40dcc-7b8a-4e12-bf76-a64696cd3f0d}} , are calculated event by event. The ratio {{formula:53cd4c3a-6ab5-4fab-99b7-d2ae937ba0e9}} accounts for the difference between {{formula:afe1721e-7352-4131-8163-9b502dc4e84e}} and {{formula:bb27097d-3576-4e16-b934-6aec52003710}} expected from initial geometry fluctuations.We have calculated the magnitude of the second and third Fourier components of two-particle azimuthal correlations and expected contributions to these components from elliptic and triangular flow for particle pairs in {{formula:b7febb91-e7be-401d-8d95-f814c9653335}} 200 GeV Au+Au collisions from AMPT within the pseudorapidity range {{formula:c3eab507-a96a-496c-a203-1ad664d2061a}} and {{formula:facee03a-2dd0-4242-ac52-b41ddb4dd842}} . The results are presented in Fig. REF as a function of number of participating nucleons. More than 80% of the third Fourier coefficient of azimuthal correlations can be accounted for by triangular flow with respect to the minor axis of triangularity. The difference between {{formula:a1a9b0da-fdbf-4f74-9c14-18e457b24dfb}} and {{formula:d9ce4cc9-ca87-463d-ae97-73cc00001b2a}} may be due to two different effects: There might be contributions from correlations other than triangular flow to {{formula:01d4d780-83eb-449d-9046-bbd7e07bb839}} or the angle with respect to which the global triangular anisotropy develops might not be given precisely by the minor axis of triangularity calculated from positions of participant nucleons, i.e. {{formula:5b7577ae-4d9b-47ff-be4c-56ac731d6948}} might be an underestimate for the magnitude of triangular flow. More detailed studies are needed to distinguish between these two effects.We have also studied the magnitudes of elliptic and triangular flow more differentially as a function of transverse momentum and number of participating nucleons in the AMPT model. Figure REF shows the results as a function of transverse momentum for particles at mid-rapidity ({{formula:e19ecc2a-befd-4217-bbd2-ecba4c6a3ab2}} ) for different ranges of number of participating nucleons. The dependence of triangular flow on transverse momentum is observed to show similar gross features as elliptic flow. A more detailed comparison can be made by taking the ratio of triangular to elliptic flow, shown in Fig. REF as a function of number of participating nucleons for different ranges of transverse momentum. The relative strength of triangular flow is observed to increase with centrality and transverse momentum. This observation is qualitatively consistent with the trends in experimentally measured ridge yield .
Triangular flow in experimental data: While AMPT reproduces the expected proportionality of {{formula:06e13178-7be8-47bb-9034-b7f52049847e}} and {{formula:2290a531-866c-4fa0-a4e7-53208a10fcd9}} , the absolute magnitude of {{formula:c1ea6775-8ae8-4c7a-958d-5e75b528169e}} is underestimated compared to data and hydrodynamic calculations. To allow a comparison of the {{formula:0374a848-30a6-4809-bf6a-6a1d75569aa9}} calculations to data, we therefore use the ratio of the third and second Fourier coefficients. For data, this ratio is given by {{formula:d66d3c3b-9fbf-477c-9610-804ceb6c36e2}}The factors {{formula:10d20cb5-d8f9-42e2-b67e-a5d0b1eb2099}} and {{formula:de105407-2328-48ca-8f3c-8c5e8dc08489}} in Eq. REF cancel out in this ratio. Results for PHOBOS , and STAR measurements are plotted as a function of number of participating nucleons in Figures REF and fig:V2OverV3VsNpartb, respectively. It is observed that {{formula:3e33b845-4b96-4cfa-a778-7a35ae75177b}} increases with centrality and with the transverse momentum of the particles.Also shown in Fig. REF is the magnitude of {{formula:6559a4ea-07cf-45e7-a36d-8f71f03b7f9e}} in the AMPT model with similar {{formula:3b8c2e5e-c5f8-47e9-b272-8b13d63f3200}} , {{formula:94a1ac31-35db-4061-abf1-7594fd8f925b}} and {{formula:da030fc9-3849-499c-b5ed-280aea7e1b95}} selections to the available experimental data. The calculations from the model show a qualitative agreement with the data in term of the dependence of {{formula:fc04b4e7-43a1-474a-a906-b0a05842e8a7}} on the pseudorapidity region, particle momenta and centrality. Since the {{formula:67832cbd-eac8-4984-895b-9643acf8024e}} component of two-particle correlations in the model is known to be mostly due to the triangular anisotropy in the initial collision geometry, this observation suggests that triangular flow may play an important role in understanding the ridge and broad away-side structures in data.A closer look at the properties of the ridge and broad away-side is possible via studies of three particle correlations. Triangular flow predicts a very distinct signature in three particle correlation measurements. Two recent publications by the STAR experiment present results on correlations in {{formula:73365517-6007-4adb-a1cb-f29e1ae52899}} -{{formula:4a27e517-8885-4769-a28c-2956176093d8}} space for {{formula:8bbeff34-76c3-4981-8566-7f0cf2827bd1}} and in {{formula:79c479b2-08ad-4f49-a7bb-ac9b295817d4}} -{{formula:1fc8ac53-7b18-4e82-9016-d349369a6f6b}} space for {{formula:cf82cfc3-6c14-45db-ba15-7b45602acade}} . In {{formula:37cec044-03e4-4caa-9377-9b9bbb258fb1}} -{{formula:23f66614-5f4b-44b1-bf54-f771b0fa131f}} space, off diagonal away-side correlations have been observed (e.g. first associated particle at {{formula:d0931562-7225-419d-88a3-66519f97166d}} and second associated particle at {{formula:7330b93b-1701-4aa8-baf7-88d8b706e3d4}} ) consistent with expectations from triangular flow. In {{formula:54689f0a-27ea-4cea-ad4f-3bb7ee349443}} -{{formula:61faadf9-9c9f-45e3-9cf7-877261a491ae}} space, no correlation structure between the two associated ridge particles was detected, also consistent with triangular flow.
Summary: We have introduced the concepts of participant triangularity and triangular flow, which quantify the triangular anisotropy in the initial and final states of heavy-ion collisions. It has been shown that inclusive and triggered two-particle azimuthal correlations at large {{formula:adda144b-561c-4ff5-ad60-9c22b59d7736}} in heavy-ion collisions are well described by the first three Fourier components. It has been demonstrated that event-by-event fluctuations lead to a finite triangularity value in Glauber Monte Carlo events and that this triangular anisotropy in the initial geometry leads to a triangular anisotropy in particle production in the AMPT model. The third Fourier coefficient of azimuthal correlations at large pseudorapidity separations have been found to be dominated by triangular flow in the model. We have studied the ratio of the third and second Fourier coefficients of azimuthal correlations in experimental data and the AMPT model as a function of centrality and pseudorapidity and momentum ranges of particle pairs. A qualitative agreement between data and model has been observed. This suggests that the ridge and broad away-side features observed in two-particle correlation measurements in Au+Au collisions contain a significant, and perhaps dominant, contribution from triangular flow. Our findings support previous evidence from measurements of the system size dependence of elliptic flow and elliptic flow fluctuations on the importance of geometric fluctuations in the initial collision region. Detailed studies of triangular flow can shed new light on the initial conditions and the collective expansion of the matter created in heavy-ion collisions.The authors acknowledge fruitful discussions with Wei Li, Constantin Loizides, Peter Steinberg and Edward Wenger. This work was supported by U.S. DOE grant DE-FG02-94ER40818.

Laboratory bounds on Lorentz symmetry violation in low energy neutrino physics
abstract: Quantitative bounds on Lorentz symmetry violation in the neutrino sector have been obtained by analyzing existing laboratory data on neutron $\beta$ decay and pion leptonic decays. In particular some parameters appearing in the energy-momentum dispersion relations for $\nu_e$ and $\nu_\mu$ have been constrained in two typical cases arising in many models accounting for Lorentz violation.
Lorentz-violating dispersion relations: In recent times ultra-high energy Lorentz symmetry violations have been investigated, both theoretically and experimentally,Hereafter for simplicity we use the term “violation” or “breakdown” of the Lorentz symmetry, but, in some theories, although Special Relativity does not hold anymore, an underlying extended Lorentz invariance exists (this happens, for example, in “Doubly” Special Relativity , , where “deformed” 4-rotation generators are considered). by means of quite different approaches, sometimes extending, sometimes abandoning the formal and conceptual framework of Einstein's Special Relativity. The most important consequence of a Lorentz violation (LV) is the modification of the ordinary momentum-energy dipersion law {{formula:a31c76f4-f9d6-45f6-861e-7d89c98f54b1}} at energy scales usually assumed of the order of the Planck mass, by means of additional terms which vanish in the low momentum limit. Lorentz-breaking observable effects appear in Grand-Unification Theories , in (Super)String/Brane theories , in Quantum Gravity , in foam-like quantum spacetimes ; in spacetimes endowed with a nontrivial topology or with a discrete structure at the Planck length , , or with a (canonical or noncanonical) noncommutative geometry , , ; in the so-called “extensions” of the Standard Model incorporating breaking of Lorentz and CPT symmetries ; in theories with a variable speed of light or variable physical constants. In particular, the M-Theory and the Loop Quantum Gravity (as in the version with semiclassical spin-network structure of spacetime) , , lead to postulate an essentially discrete, quantized spacetime, where a fundamental mass-energy scale naturally arises, in addition to {{formula:7a30e268-afe6-40f2-ad25-ca4e22892fcd}} and {{formula:c0a90477-18e8-4f49-853f-97a507768b9e}} . An intrinsic length is directly correlated to the existence of a “cut-off” in the transferred momentum necessary to avoid the occurrence of “UV catastrophes” in Quantum Field Theories. Other divergences, as those emerging in black hole entropy, could possibly be averted in the presence of quantum LV's.A natural extension of the standard dispersion law can be put in most cases under the general form ({{formula:e392dc53-921b-4005-9f6c-deac9c43230c}} ) {{formula:c2db0f44-d2a7-47f3-bc3b-99acb6de3343}}where {{formula:0855f43f-61d9-4104-a5e4-cbcef008d173}} indicates a (large) mass scale characterizing LV. By using a series expansion for {{formula:fda7420b-2d3d-4440-b70c-cf5ae9903bc9}} , under the assumption being {{formula:9e8f7cf7-9f1b-4aab-b2ca-dbfecfc6f5e9}} a very large quantity, we can consider only the lower order nonzero term in the expansion: {{formula:a742fcbc-d455-4581-805a-17c882292a38}}The most recurring exponent in the literature on LV is {{formula:0b83f476-6bfd-4b6a-83b6-a178f81ab3cf}} : {{formula:e1473091-4c82-4e80-acca-925bf2da5708}}Indeed in “noncritical”-Liouville String Theory , we have {{formula:1f8c38cd-beef-40b6-aa89-3dec8ca67836}} , where {{formula:7ad400b3-e75f-4c61-837a-f40c9e2f6dbd}} is the string coupling and {{formula:d6c9dcb9-70e8-4b09-bbc9-397a98a9633c}} is a string (non-Planck) mass scale; similar expressions are obtained in the Standard Model extensions of Colladay and Kostelecký, as well as in theories with a spacetime “medium” or quantum foam, or even in Quantum Gravity. We find dispersion laws analogous to the {{formula:c1605381-7678-4e10-9306-987f1f423454}} case in Deformed or Doubly Special Relativity, , , working in k-deformed Lie-algebra noncommutative (k-Minkowski) spacetimes, in which both the Planck scale and the speed of light act as characteristic scales of a 6-parameter group of spacetime 4-rotations with deformed but preserved Lorentz symmetries. For example, in where the Lie algebra is given by {{formula:4acf7da1-bf7b-43c5-84d9-9a7409055684}} , {{formula:8ee5fc5d-edbd-4908-90ff-472615f2e1f4}} ({{formula:ff828c52-800f-4a4a-8191-52e601164450}} being a very small length which may eventually be taken of the order of {{formula:4e82c975-9e12-4dbd-9f0e-6fce6b5ac2ce}} ) we have {{formula:117e6bcf-daa9-4635-ab85-48e073488e15}}which for {{formula:467f9d78-fd27-4fdc-91d0-3bb7249e1dc5}} and considering terms up to O({{formula:b9683a42-9c5d-438f-a10c-c1ce3e365da0}} ) reduces to the above seen cubic form {{formula:56c9e3e6-e316-45eb-9d8f-74bb18fdf7b9}}The {{formula:40d71134-72f7-46f5-b814-16e49db97369}} case in (REF ) has been studied by Coleman and Glashow , while {{formula:3fd8f69b-7400-472a-a6af-674fbee4cc88}} is found for some “Quantum Deformed Poincaré Groups” , in spacetime foam scenarios at low energies , and in effective field theories including Lorentz violating dimension-5 operators , . Different dispersion laws arise from canonical Loop Gravity, Supergravity, or String Theory critical in B-background , , and from canonical noncommutative geometries . For example in , assuming the canonical commutation relation {{formula:c26836d5-bba0-448e-a6af-9cc4985fea59}} we find {{formula:4b82a438-044c-4089-b61a-2991b696ca7f}}Other important models, which assume the Planck 4-momentum (a new Lorentz invariant besides the speed of light) as a frame-independent quantity, entail a linear behavior (i.e., {{formula:d1081645-bc6d-4bf1-93e9-130cf89e69a7}} ): in , , , , for {{formula:da91a866-1cc2-45fb-9709-d6f690976bfd}} and up to terms of the order of {{formula:83335520-0aab-47b4-b2a2-ab5be90f9ef4}} , {{formula:a1cbb14e-ad6e-405e-b42f-a49e6f5418a1}} driving the mass scale of the Lorentz breakdown, we have {{formula:0cb06fe4-1f12-4432-aa49-07e88af329e8}}In such models it is interesting to study the (small momenta) nonrelativistic limit wherein the nonstandard linear term rules.As pointed out by Bertolami , evidences of violation of the Lorentz symmetry seem to emerge from the observation of: a) ultra-high energy cosmic rays with energies beyond the Greisen-Zatsepin-Kuzmin cut-off (of the order of {{formula:ec8c071d-0848-4d80-8300-9e0fdfd70126}} eV); b) gamma rays with energies beyond 20 TeV from distant sources such as Markarian 421 and Markarian 501 blazars ; c) longitudinal evolution of air showers produced by ultra-high energy hadronic particles which seem to suggest that pions are more stable than expected .The theoretical applications of the modified dispersion relation do carry many threshold effects associated to asymmetric momenta in photoproduction, pair creation, photon stability, vacuum Čerenkov effects, etc. , , long baseline dispersion and vacuum birefringence (signals from gamma ray bursts, active galactic nuclei, pulsars) , dynamical effects of LV background fields (gravitational coupling and additional wave modes) , different maximum speeds for different particles , .An interesting consequence of a nonstandard dispersion law is an essentially “non-Newtonian” dynamics at very high energies.In fact, if {{formula:c9c38c25-6f80-46da-af43-fdf5480d6a58}} , from {{formula:042262ed-3af0-43c7-914a-ca1ebadd9a91}} we have not {{formula:4a892ea8-1dd8-479e-8978-8ed66aff2da9}} . Thus, in four-dimensional notation, we have {{formula:ad296c9e-3cfc-4302-80bb-69c42e1cc993}} and, time-differentiating both sides, {{formula:fc13f0ce-b51c-4f49-8a28-ad86b9465f7c}} ({{formula:c963b67c-4b6f-412d-b32e-1fd0cd9f5fdf}} indicating the 4-force): Newton's Law does not hold anymore. All that recalls the non-Newtonian features of spinning particles dynamics at the Compton scale {{formula:344f68dd-b72b-4feb-a4a3-1ab61ae509c7}} .There are further deep experimental implications at low energy , , , mostly in the Standard Model extensions based on a Lagrangian containing any possible phenomenologically relevant Lorentz- and CPT-violating term, for hadrons , nucleons , electrons , photons , muons .Consequences on the absorption and the spectrum of gamma rays and on the synchrotron radiation have been investigated in noncommutative QED as well.Allen and Yokoo list a series of proposed or performed experiments, on Earth and in space, to test both Lorentz and CPT symmetries: atomic experiments , , (penning trap experiments with electrons, protons and their respective antiparticles, clock comparison experiments exploiting Zeeman and hyperfine transitions, spin polarized torsion pendulum experiments, etc.); clock-based experiments to probe effects of variations in both orientation and velocity (employing H-masers, laser-cooled Cs and Rb clocks, dual nuclear Zeeman He-Xe masers, superconducting microwave cavity oscillators); experiments involving neutrino and kaon oscillations ; measurements of cosmological birefringence by interferometric searches of spacetime metric fluctuations .Breakdown of the relativistic invariance and deformed “mass shell” relation have been advanced, above all, for neutrinos because of their very small mass and very large momenta. Consequences of a nonstandard dispersion law on neutrino oscillations has been analyzed in and ; the observable effects of the non-Lorentz nature of the flavour eigenstates have been investigated by Blasone et al. , by Klinkhamer and by Smolin and Maguejio . In particular, striking effects on neutrino physics are expected , , , as a possible explanation of the arrival delays of neutrinos emitted from supernova SN1987A, as well as a possible kinematical stability of neutrons and pions of very high momentum. As stated in , , SN1987A might constitute an interesting laboratory for studying LV's, because of the relatively high energy of the observed neutrinos (up to 100MeV), the relatively large distances travelled (about {{formula:a26fba0e-af41-4957-ac7c-ad21fd454c51}} light-years), and the short (of the order of second) duration of the bursts.By assuming the dispersion relation (REF ) for the mass eigenstates of electron neutrinos, Carmona and Cortes succeed in explaining the so-called “tritium beta-decay anomaly", i.e., the anomalous excess of decay events near the endpoint of the electron energy spectrum (where nonrelativistic few-eV neutrinos are produced) which yields a characteristic “tail” in the Kurie plot. If the Special Relativity dispersion law holds, this unexpected phenomenon involves negative squared masses for electron neutrinos. Instead, using eq. (REF ), those authors show that the excess of electron events is only apparent and no tachyonic neutrino is needed. In the same paper the Lorentz violating momentum-energy relation is exploited to account for the spread of arrival times of neutrinos from SN1987A, and for the stability of very high energy neutrons and pions in cosmic rays, since the high momentum decay reactions {{formula:9d5a198e-321b-495f-88df-a92f5b5beb43}} , {{formula:38636f2c-5e00-4462-8570-229273ba43e5}} become forbidden.In the next sections we shall deduce upper bounds on the LV parameters appearing in equations (REF ) and (REF ), respectively, by using the available precision experimental data on the neutron lifetime and the decay rates of the charged {{formula:0cc0e5f9-9c7f-4172-aff8-02643b7625f1}} meson.We assume that even if the kinematics of the above decays is affected by the LV in the dispersion law, nevertheless the dynamics, at the low energy scales of laboratory experiments, is essentially the one given by the Standard Model. If other radiative contributions (as additional Feynman diagrams) should appear as a consequence of the modified mass shell, we expected they are irrelevant at the considered energy scales, due to the excellent experimental verification of the Standard Model predictions at these scales.
