Pion leptonic decay: Since long time the ratio of the decay rates of the charged {{formula:23c55660-be9d-49dd-bc8c-a7251ca921aa}} meson, {{formula:2c65ff3a-4003-4012-a0a1-b7ec0a34d576}}has been used to test fundamental properties of elementary particle physics; this quantity in fact at a first, good approximation, is independent of the details of {{formula:4563815c-f15d-4d7d-a6b3-55eb56da4552}} meson interactions . Assuming that the dynamics of the considered decays is substantially described in the framework of the Standard Model, the ratio in (REF ) can also be useful to obtain constraints on the Lorentz-breaking parameters in the neutrino dispersion relations. Note that, on the contrary to what happens for the neutron decay, the single decay rates (instead of their ratio) cannot give useful informations on these parameters, since the experiments measuring directly the above mentioned processes are used to fix the value of the pion decay constant {{formula:06a74eca-2b0f-4996-9f7f-a186e1ea2f79}} . The decay rate of a charged pion into a lepton-neutrino pair can be written as : {{formula:79b8157c-55dd-4ab9-a4e4-8577c51da573}}where {{formula:1039d981-101b-4536-8b1b-64ed63bdadf4}} is the Cabibbo angle. Assuming the dispersion relation in Eq. (REF ) (with {{formula:24490682-957a-448f-b26f-bd26229b025b}} ) for neutrinos, the integral in Eq. (REF ) evaluates to: {{formula:f654fa2a-7190-424b-aa81-29e03a9b9c2d}}Thus, at the first order in small quantities, the decay rate takes the form: {{formula:ff614253-bf5f-4fe7-b788-852b5d0481f8}}where {{formula:3b195788-c7aa-4d6e-b8c3-7af8522073aa}}indicated the standard Born rate . A precise determination of the {{formula:95f8a62b-248a-4885-8839-2cfd7d6e61a3}} parameter again would require the computation of the radiative corrections (see for example ) but, as in the neutron decay case, we are interested only in an order of magnitude estimate, so that we will now proceed as in the previous section. The ratio in Eq. (REF ) is, then, w